1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,722
I never really planned to be a lifeboat volunteer when I came to live

2
00:00:03,722 --> 00:00:05,110
in Northsea.

3
00:00:05,110 --> 00:00:08,463
I’d been working in London as a website designer,

4
00:00:08,463 --> 00:00:12,580
but although that was interesting, I didn’t like city life.

5
00:00:12,580 --> 00:00:15,893
I’d been really keen on boats as a teenager,

6
00:00:15,893 --> 00:00:19,146
and I thought if I went to live by the sea, I might be

7
00:00:19,146 --> 00:00:22,760
able to pursue that interest a bit more in my free time.

8
00:00:22,760 --> 00:00:26,360
Then I found that the Lifeboat Institution was looking for volunteers,

9
00:00:26,360 --> 00:00:29,840
so I decided to apply.

10
00:00:29,840 --> 00:00:36,053
The Lifeboat Institution building here in Northsea’s hard to miss, it’s one of the

11
00:00:36,053 --> 00:00:37,300
largest in the country.

12
00:00:37,300 --> 00:00:41,739
It was built 15 years ago with funds provided by a generous member of

13
00:00:41,739 --> 00:00:44,430
the public who’d lived here all her life.

14
00:00:44,430 --> 00:00:48,546
As the Lifeboat Institution is a charity that relies on that kind of donation,

15
00:00:48,546 --> 00:00:51,418
rather than funding provided by the government,

16
00:00:51,418 --> 00:00:54,410
that was a huge help to us.

17
00:00:54,410 --> 00:00:58,165
When I applied, I had to have a health assessment.

18
00:00:58,165 --> 00:01:01,800
The doctors were particularly interested in my vision.

19
00:01:01,800 --> 00:01:03,483
I used to be short-sighted,

20
00:01:03,483 --> 00:01:05,838
so I’d had to wear glasses,

21
00:01:05,838 --> 00:01:10,550
but I’d had laser eye surgery two years earlier so that was OK.

22
00:01:10,550 --> 00:01:15,468
They gave me tests for colour blindness and they thought I might have a

23
00:01:15,468 --> 00:01:16,171
problem there,

24
00:01:16,171 --> 00:01:18,630
but it turned out I was OK.

25
00:01:18,630 --> 00:01:22,998
When the coastguard gets an alert, all the volunteers are contacted

26
00:01:22,998 --> 00:01:25,380
and rush to the lifeboat station.

27
00:01:25,380 --> 00:01:31,676
Our target’s to get there in five minutes, then we try to get the boat off the dock

28
00:01:31,676 --> 00:01:34,990
and out to sea in another six to eight minutes.

29
00:01:34,990 --> 00:01:41,652
Our team’s proud that we usually achieve that – the average time across the country’s eight

30
00:01:41,652 --> 00:01:43,220
and a half minutes.

31
00:01:43,220 --> 00:01:47,190
I’ve recently qualified as what’s called a ‘helmsman’,

32
00:01:47,190 --> 00:01:51,160
which means I have the ultimate responsibility for the lifeboat.

33
00:01:51,160 --> 00:01:55,351
I have to check that the equipment we use is in working order –

34
00:01:55,351 --> 00:01:59,865
the crew have special life jackets that can support up to four people in

35
00:01:59,865 --> 00:02:00,510
the water.

36
00:02:00,510 --> 00:02:05,240
And it’s ultimately my decision whether it’s safe to launch the boat.

37
00:02:05,240 --> 00:02:09,890
But it’s very rare not to launch it, even in the worst weather.

38
00:02:09,890 --> 00:02:14,840
As well as going out on the lifeboat, my work involves other things too.

39
00:02:14,840 --> 00:02:19,303
A lot of people underestimate how quickly conditions can change at sea,

40
00:02:19,303 --> 00:02:26,370
so I speak to youth groups and sailing clubs in the area about the sorts of problems that sailors

41
00:02:26,370 --> 00:02:30,090
and swimmers can have if the weather suddenly gets bad.

42
00:02:30,090 --> 00:02:35,098
We also have a lot of volunteers who organise activities to raise money for

43
00:02:35,098 --> 00:02:35,456
us,

44
00:02:35,456 --> 00:02:37,960
and we couldn’t manage without them.

45
00:02:37,960 --> 00:02:43,588
The training we get is a continuous process, focusing on technical competence

46
00:02:43,588 --> 00:02:45,464
and safe handling techniques,

47
00:02:45,464 --> 00:02:52,030
and it’s given me the confidence to deal with extreme situations without panicking.

48
00:02:52,030 --> 00:02:57,285
I was glad I’d done a first aid course before I started, as that’s

49
00:02:57,285 --> 00:03:00,570
a big help with the casualty care activities we do.

50
00:03:00,570 --> 00:03:04,246
We’ve done a lot on how to deal with ropes

51
00:03:04,246 --> 00:03:06,920
and tie knots –that’s an essential skill.

52
00:03:06,920 --> 00:03:12,640
After a year, I did a one-week residential course, led by specialists.

53
00:03:12,640 --> 00:03:16,600
They had a wave-tank where they could create extreme weather conditions –

54
00:03:16,600 --> 00:03:21,640
so we could get experience at what to do if the boat turned over

55
00:03:21,640 --> 00:03:24,160
in a storm at night, for example.

56
00:03:24,160 --> 00:03:29,942
Since I started, I’ve had to deal with a range of emergency situations.

57
00:03:29,942 --> 00:03:32,420
But the work’s hugely motivating.

58
00:03:32,420 --> 00:03:38,467
It’s not just about saving lives – I’ve learned a lot about the technology

59
00:03:38,467 --> 00:03:38,870
involved.

60
00:03:38,870 --> 00:03:41,950
My background in IT’s been useful here,

61
00:03:41,950 --> 00:03:45,800
and I can use my expertise to help other volunteers.

62
00:03:45,800 --> 00:03:49,388
They’re a great group – we’re like a family really,

63
00:03:49,388 --> 00:03:53,954
which helps when you’re dragging yourself out of bed on a cold stormy

64
00:03:53,954 --> 00:03:54,280
night.

65
00:03:54,280 --> 00:03:59,066
But actually, it’s the colder months that can be the most rewarding time.

66
00:03:59,066 --> 00:04:02,485
That’s when the incidents tend to be more serious,

67
00:04:02,485 --> 00:04:06,930
and you realise that you can make a huge difference to the outcome.

68
00:04:06,930 --> 00:04:12,140
So if any of you listeners are interested.

69
00:04:12,140 --> 00:04:12,190
.

70
00:04:12,190 --> 00:04:12,240
.
